/// A user's Gmail read for an assistant, as the native tools `GmailLabels`,
/// `GmailSearch`, `GmailRead`, `GmailThread`, `GmailAttachment` and
/// `GmailBody`, each answered by `api-request` calls against the
/// `oauth_provider` the instance names, stamped with the calling
/// conversation's owner and compacted to what a model needs. The user's token
/// never reaches this component; `api-request` attaches it host-side. Every
/// call is nested under a request: an instance whose clauses say
/// `user = "*"` cannot call on its own account, so a caller with no user of
/// its own is answered `denied`. Its calls are Gmail's
/// `labels.list`, `labels.get`, `messages.list`, `messages.get`,
/// `threads.get` and `messages.attachments.get`, all under the Gmail read
/// scope set.
///
/// Every capability is load-bearing: `config` names the provider and its
/// host, without which no call can be addressed; `store` carries each
/// request's state between the activation that issues its nested call and the
/// one that receives the answer; `tools` is the authority to call
/// `api-request`; `provides` is what makes the tools exist; `blobs` is how
/// `GmailAttachment` and `GmailBody` read Gmail's answer, asked for as an
/// attachment, and hand back the files they make of it as their own; `log`
/// records each step's tool, status and size — never a body, a header or a
/// query.
///
/// The instance's `config = { ... };` map is flat: `provider` is the
/// `oauth_provider` name; `gmail_host` is the `host[:port]` Gmail is called
/// on, which `api-request` matches against that provider's `api_hosts` and
/// the instance's clauses must admit. A missing or malformed key fails every
/// activation, naming the key.
///
/// Size the instance's result inbox with a `channel at
/// "brenn:tool-results/<slug>"` block: each live request has at most one
/// nested call outstanding, so the window must hold Σ max_concurrency results,
/// twice over for a re-delivered result — `retain_depth ≥ 2 × Σ`, rounded up
/// to a power of two. This class's Σ is 14, so 32 holds it; a tool joining
/// the class that takes Σ past 16 needs the block raised, and the family
/// default of 16 is too small for any class whose Σ is above 8.
///
/// Every nested answer crosses the bus as a tool result, so Google's body — a
/// JSON string inside `api-request`'s value, inside the result envelope — must
/// fit `[messaging] max_body_bytes` (64 KiB default). Either raise that cap to
/// carry the OAuth provider's `max_response_bytes` (256 KiB default) with a
/// margin for escaping, `messaging { max_body_bytes = 393216; }`, or lower
/// `max_response_bytes` to fit the default cap, at the cost of most HTML mail.
/// Half again covers message bodies, which are base64url and do not grow when
/// escaped; sender-written headers dense in quotes or backslashes escape to
/// about twice their size, so such a read can pass the cap below the read
/// limit.
/// An answer over the bus cap never reaches this component as Google's answer;
/// the tools treat that refusal as they treat an answer cut at the read limit.
component GoogleGmailRead {
  abi = processor;
  requires = [log, store, config, tools, provides, blobs];
  in tool-results;
  in tool-requests;
  provide gmail-labels {
    mcp_name = "GmailLabels";
    description = "The user's Gmail labels. Without `id`, every label with its `id`, `name` and `type` (`system` or `user`), in Google's order; Google gives no counts in a listing. With `id` (a label's `id`, such as `INBOX` or `Label_12`), that one label with its `messages_total`, `messages_unread`, `threads_total` and `threads_unread`, each when Google gives it. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Gmail read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$" }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 2;
  }
  provide gmail-search {
    mcp_name = "GmailSearch";
    description = "Search the user's Gmail with Gmail's search syntax (`q`, as in Gmail's search box: `from:alice@example.com is:unread newer_than:7d`). Answers `messages`, newest first as Google orders them, one line per message: `id`, `thread_id`, `from`, `subject` and `date` (the header values as sent, each when present; a value over 1000 bytes (UTF-8) is cut, ends in `…`, and the line's `headers_truncated` lists the fields cut), `internal_date` (when Gmail received it, RFC 3339 UTC), `snippet` (Gmail's preview of the text) and `labels` (user labels by name, system labels such as `INBOX` and `UNREAD` by id); `result_size_estimate` (Google's estimate of all matches); `next_page` when there are more — pass it back as `page` with the same `q`; and `unavailable`, the listed messages that could not be read, each `id` and `reason`: deleted before it could be read, or its read was cut at the provider's read limit or refused by the host (a reason naming neither `max_response_bytes` nor `max_body_bytes` is a transient fault: searching again may read it); the page's other messages are answered. `max` is how many messages to return, 1 to 25, default 10; each costs one call to Google. `spam_trash: true` searches spam and trash too. Bodies are not included. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Gmail read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "q": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 1024, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "max": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1, "maximum": 25 }, "page": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 4096, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "spam_trash": { "type": "boolean" }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["q"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 4;
  }
  provide gmail-read {
    mcp_name = "GmailRead";
    description = "Read one Gmail message by `id` (a message `id` from `GmailSearch`). Answers `id`, `thread_id`, `labels` (user labels by name, system labels such as `INBOX` by id), the headers `from`, `to`, `cc`, `bcc`, `reply_to`, `subject`, `date`, `message_id` and `in_reply_to` (each as sent, when present; `to`, `cc`, `bcc` and `reply_to` are cut over 4000 bytes (UTF-8), the other headers over 1000; a cut value ends in `…` and `headers_truncated` lists the fields cut), `internal_date` (when Gmail received it, RFC 3339 UTC), `text` (the body as text: of a plain and an HTML alternative the plain one, parts of a mixed message joined by a blank line; HTML is read as its text, content it hides included — tags, styles and scripts dropped, a link's text kept without its address, table cells separated by tabs), `text_source` (`text/plain`, `text/html`, `mixed`, or `none` when the message has no text part), `text_chars` (the whole text's length in characters), `attachments` (each `filename`, `mime_type`, `size` in bytes, `inline` — shown in the body rather than attached — and `part_id` when `GmailAttachment` can fetch it; an attachment's `filename` or `mime_type` over 1000 bytes (UTF-8) is cut and ends in `…`), and `decoding_notes` when something could not be decoded exactly (an unsupported charset, invalid bytes). `max_chars` (200 to 60000, default 12000) bounds `text`: `text` is cut at `max_chars` characters, and shorter when the whole answer would otherwise exceed the wrapper's result ceiling; `text_truncated` and `text_chars` say so either way. A message too large to read whole — larger than the provider's read limit, or refused by the host — is answered without its body: `text` is null, `text_omitted` says why, and `snippet` (Gmail's preview) stands in for it; a reason naming neither `max_response_bytes` nor `max_body_bytes` is a transient fault, and reading it again may answer the body. `GmailBody` answers the whole text, the HTML as sent and the HTML as text as files, above these cuts. To read a thread, read its messages one by one. An attachment listed with a `part_id` is fetched with `GmailAttachment`; one listed without is carried inside the message or nested deeper than `GmailAttachment` reads. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Gmail read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$" }, "max_chars": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 200, "maximum": 60000 }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["id"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 4;
  }
  provide gmail-thread {
    mcp_name = "GmailThread";
    description = "One Gmail thread's messages by `id` (a `thread_id` from `GmailSearch` or `GmailRead`), oldest first, without bodies. Answers `id`, `subject` (the first message's), `message_count` (how many messages the thread holds), `messages_omitted` when any are left out (`messages_omitted` counts the messages left out — older than the newest `max`, or past the wrapper's result ceiling), and `messages`, each with `id`, `from`, `to`, `cc`, `subject` (only when it differs from the thread's), `date` (the header values as sent, each when present; `to` and `cc` are cut over 4000 bytes (UTF-8), the other headers over 1000; a cut value ends in `…` and `headers_truncated` lists the fields cut — on the message, or on the thread for the thread's `subject`), `internal_date` (when Gmail received it, RFC 3339 UTC), `snippet` (Gmail's preview of the text) and `labels` (user labels by name, system labels such as `INBOX` by id). `max` is how many of the newest messages to return, 1 to 100, default 30. The whole thread is read before `max` applies. A thread too large to read whole is read again without `to` and `cc`, and `recipients_omitted` says why; a thread whose `from`, `subject` and `date` alone are too large is refused as `internal` (naming the provider's `max_response_bytes` or the bus's `max_body_bytes`), and a smaller `max` does not help. A `recipients_omitted` or `internal` reason naming neither is a transient fault: reading the thread again may answer it whole. Read a message's text with `GmailRead`. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Gmail read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$" }, "max": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1, "maximum": 100 }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["id"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 2;
  }
  provide gmail-attachment {
    mcp_name = "GmailAttachment";
    description = "Fetch one attachment of a Gmail message: `id` is the message's `id` and `part_id` the attachment's `part_id` as `GmailRead` lists it. An attachment `GmailRead` lists without a `part_id` cannot be fetched, and a part carried inside the message is refused: read it with `GmailRead`, or whole with `GmailBody` when it is body text. Answers `id`, `part_id`, `filename` and `mime_type` (as the sender gave them; over 1000 bytes (UTF-8) cut and ending in `…`), `size` (the file's length in bytes) and `attachment`, the name of the result's attachment that holds the file: forward it with `BrennSend`, save it with `brenn-tool save`, or hand it to another tool. An attachment over 50000000 bytes, or whose encoded form exceeds the host's largest blob, is refused. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Gmail read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$" }, "part_id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9.]{1,64}$" }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["id", "part_id"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 1;
  }
  provide gmail-body {
    mcp_name = "GmailBody";
    description = "A Gmail message's body whole, as files: `id` is the message's `id` (a message `id` from `GmailSearch`) and `forms` one to three of `text` (the body as text, exactly `GmailRead`'s `text` uncut: of a plain and an HTML alternative the plain one, parts of a mixed message joined by a blank line, an HTML-only message's HTML read as text), `html` (the message's HTML as sent, every HTML part decoded to UTF-8 whatever its charset, so a `<meta>` naming another charset no longer matches; several HTML parts are joined by a newline, one document after another) and `rendered` (that HTML read as text, as `GmailRead` reads it). A plain-text message has no `html` or `rendered`. Answers `id` and, under each form asked for, either `attachment` (the name of the result's attachment holding the form, UTF-8 `text/plain` for `text` and `rendered`, `text/html` for `html`) with `size` in bytes, `source` for `text` (`text/plain`, `text/html` or `mixed`) and `parts` for `html` and `rendered` (how many HTML parts), or `absent` saying why there is none (`no text part`, `no HTML part`, or `empty`). Forward a file with `BrennSend`, save it with `brenn-tool save`, or hand it to another tool. `decoding_notes`, when present, says what could not be decoded exactly (an unsupported charset, invalid bytes shown as U+FFFD) and how many text parts Gmail carries as attachment references, which are in no form: `GmailRead` lists them under `attachments`, and one listed with a `part_id` is fetched with `GmailAttachment`. A message whose answer from Gmail is over 786432 bytes is refused: `GmailRead` answers its text cut, and a caller granted `api-request` reads the whole message with `format=raw` and `response_attachment` (`docs/providers/google.md`). A message whose answer is over the host's largest blob is refused too, and `GmailRead` answers its text cut. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Gmail read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$" }, "forms": { "type": "array", "minItems": 1, "maxItems": 3, "uniqueItems": true, "items": { "type": "string", "enum": ["text", "html", "rendered"] } }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["id", "forms"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 1;
  }
}
